Q: How is Quarkus similar to Spring Boot, and what’s different?

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Similarities (your Spring skills transfer)

| Area              | Both have                                      |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| Goal              | Fast microservice/API development              |
| REST APIs         | Annotation-based controllers/resources         |
| DI / IoC          | Container injects dependencies                 |
| Config            | application.properties / profiles              |
| Persistence       | Hibernate / JPA style data access              |
| Transactions      | `@Transactional` concept                       |
| Messaging         | Kafka / AMQP integrations                      |
| Security          | JWT / OIDC style auth extensions               |
| Testing           | `@QuarkusTest` ≈ `@SpringBootTest` idea        |
| Cloud             | Docker/K8s friendly, health/metrics            |
| Starters          | Spring starters ≈ Quarkus extensions           |

Mental model:
If you know “Controller → Service → Repository → DB” in Spring,
Quarkus is “Resource → Service → Panache entity/repo → DB”.

────────────────────────────────────────
❌ Key differences

| Topic                | Spring Boot                         | Quarkus                                  |
| -------------------- | ----------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| Philosophy           | Runtime flexibility / reflection    | Build-time augmentation                  |
| Bootstrap            | `@SpringBootApplication` + run()    | Generated main; no Spring-style bootstrap annotation |
| DI                   | Spring IoC / `@Autowired`           | CDI / `@Inject`                          |
| Bean stereotypes     | `@Service` `@Component`             | CDI scopes e.g. `@ApplicationScoped`     |
| REST                 | Spring MVC `@RestController`        | JAX-RS `@Path` `@GET`                    |
| Data access sugar    | Spring Data JPA repositories        | Panache (Active Record or Repository)    |
| Reactive types       | Mono/Flux (Reactor)                 | Uni/Multi (Mutiny)                       |
| Actuator             | `/actuator/*`                       | `/q/health`, `/q/metrics`, etc.          |
| Native images        | Improving; historically heavier     | First-class GraalVM native focus         |
| Ecosystem breadth    | Huge (Batch, Integration, Security…) | Smaller but strong cloud-native set     |
| Startup / memory     | Higher (typical JVM apps)           | Lower (esp. native / optimized JVM)      |

Biggest interview sentence:
“Spring Boot does a lot at **runtime** (scan, auto-config, proxies).
Quarkus moves much of that to **build time**, so runtime is leaner.”

────────────────────────────────────────
When choose which?

Choose Quarkus when:
- K8s density / cold start / scale-to-zero matters
- New services, team OK learning CDI/JAX-RS
- Native compilation is a goal

Choose Spring Boot when:
- Existing Spring codebase/team expertise (like you)
- Need broad Spring ecosystem (Batch, complex Security customizations, etc.)
- Peak long-running throughput is fine on JVM (gap shrinks after warm-up)

────────────────────────────────────────
Migration note

Quarkus offers Spring compatibility extensions (spring-di/web/data-jpa) for porting,
but idiomatic Quarkus = CDI + JAX-RS + Panache.

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview answer

“Quarkus and Spring Boot both build REST microservices with DI, config, and JPA.
They’re similar in architecture layers. The difference is Quarkus optimizes for containers by doing bean/config/metadata work
at build time and using CDI/JAX-RS/Panache instead of Spring MVC/Data.
I’d pick Quarkus for new cloud-native services needing fast start/low memory; Spring Boot when the team/ecosystem is already Spring-centric.”
